.SH "NAME"
nvme-keys-insert-tls \- Insert a NVMe TLS PSK into a keyring
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fInvme\fR [<global\-options>] \fIkeys insert\-tls\fR [\-\-keyring=<name> | \-k <name>]
                        [\-\-keytype=<type> | \-t <type>]
                        [\-\-hostnqn=<nqn> | \-n <nqn>]
                        [\-\-subsysnqn=<nqn> | \-c <nqn>]
                        [\-\-keydata=<key> | \-d <key>]
                        [\-\-identity=<id\-vers> | \-I <id\-vers>]
                        [\-\-compat | \-C]
                        [\-\-keyfile=<keyfile> | \-f <keyfile>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Takes a NVMe TLS PSK in the PSK interchange format \fINVMeTLSkey\-1:01:<base64\-encoded data>:\fR, \fBderives\fR a \fIretained\fR key identity from it and the given host/subsystem NQNs using the HKDF\-Expand\-Label algorithm from RFC 8446, and stores the result into the specified keyring\&. The \fIretained\fR key is stored with the identity \fINVMe0R0<hmac> <host NQN> <subsystem NQN>\fR (for identity version \fI0\fR) or \fINVMe1R0<hmac> <host NQN> <subsystem NQN> <PSK hash>\fR (for identity version \fI1\fR)\&. Once the \fIretained\fR key is stored in the keyring the original key material cannot be retrieved\&.
.sp
This command is for deriving a \fBnew\fR retained\-key identity from raw PSK material; it always needs a subsystem NQN (and, if not given, will look up the host NQN) to compute that identity\&. If the identity under which a key should be stored is already known \(em for example a key previously exported by linknvme\-keys\-export[1], or a KX\-HMAC\-CHAP secret \(em use linknvme\-keys\-import[1] instead, which inserts a key under an explicit identity without deriving one\&.
.sp
If \fI\-\-keydata\fR is not given the key is read from stdin, which also allows inserting a key stored in a file, e\&.g\&. \fInvme keys insert\-tls \-c <subsysnqn> < keyfile\fR\&.
.sp
To check whether a key is already loaded without inserting it, use linknvme\-keys\-check\-tls[1]\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\-k <name>, \-\-keyring=<name>
.RS 4
Name of the keyring into which the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key should be stored\&. Default is
\fI\&.nvme\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-t <type>, \-\-keytype=<type>
.RS 4
Type of the key for resulting TLS key\&. Default is
\fIpsk\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-n <nqn>, \-\-hostnqn=<nqn>
.RS 4
Host NVMe Qualified Name (NQN) to be used to derive the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key\&.
.RE
.PP
\-c <nqn>, \-\-subsysnqn=<nqn>
.RS 4
Subsystem NVMe Qualified Name (NQN) to be used to derive the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key\&. Required\&.
.RE
.PP
\-d <key>, \-\-keydata=<key>
.RS 4
Key to be inserted\&. If not given, the key is read from stdin\&.
.RE
.PP
\-I <id\-vers>, \-\-identity=<id\-vers>
.RS 4
NVMe TLS key identity version to be used;
\fI0\fR
for the default identity, and
\fI1\fR
for the TLS identity suffixed by the PSK hash as specified in TP8018\&.
.RE
.PP
\-C, \-\-compat
.RS 4
Use the original non\-RFC 8446 compliant algorithm when deriving the TLS key for compatibility with older implementations\&.
.RE
.PP
\-f <keyfile>, \-\-keyfile=<keyfile>
.RS 4
Append the resulting TLS key to keyfile\&.
.RE
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.sp
The following options are defined at the top\-level nvme command and are available to this subcommand:
.PP
\-\-dry\-run
.RS 4
Print the command that would be executed, but do not actually execute it\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-no\-ioctl\-probing
.RS 4
Disable probing for 64\-bit IOCTL support\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-no\-retries
.RS 4
Disable retry logic on transient errors\&.
.RE
.PP
\-o <fmt>, \-\-output\-format=<fmt>
.RS 4
Set the reporting format to
\fInormal\fR,
\fItabular, \*(Aqjson\fR, or
\fIbinary\fR\&. Only one output format may be used at a time\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-output\-format\-version=<version>
.RS 4
Select the output format version\&. Version
\fI1\fR
uses the original field naming, while version
\fI2\fR
(default) provides more consistent and script\-friendly field names\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-timeout=<ms>
.RS 4
Set the timeout for the command in milliseconds\&.
.RE
.PP
\-v, \-\-verbose
.RS 4
Increase the level of detail in the output\&. May be specified multiple times to further increase verbosity\&.
.RE
.sp
These options can also be set as machine\-wide defaults in nvme\-cli\&.conf(5)\&. A command\-line flag always overrides the file\&.
